          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Arithmetic cryptography is the developing subject that describes
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-key_cryptography"} public key
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cryptography"} cryptography
            systems based on the use of arithmetic geometry of schemes (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+spaces"} global analytic spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          %p There are two well known examples of such systems:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The first and most used one, which is very efficient
                %strong because
                it is conformal to the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KISS_principle"} KISS principle
            %li
              %p
                The second one is based on the
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_logarithm"} discrete logarithm
                problem on elliptic curves (or more generally abelian varieties) over finite fields. It has the advantage on the first algorithm of allowing to make shorter keys, without compromising security (NSA has generalized the use of
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve_cryptography"} elliptic curve cryptography
                algorithm both for commercial and classified use, as explained on the wikipedia page on elliptic curve cryptography).
          %p
            The basic idea of arithmetic cryptography is to use a finite family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of polynomials with integer coefficients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1,\dots,P_m\in \mathbb{Z}[X_1,\dots,X_n]
            (or more generally a quasi-projective scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of finite type over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+space"} global analytic space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            over a convenient Banach ring), encoded in a finite number of integers (the coefficients and degrees of the corresponding polynomials), together with some additional data (such as a way to cut a part of the associated motive) to define a
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-key_cryptography"} public key cryptosystem
          %p Some computational aspects of general motives have been investigated in the case of motives of modular forms by Bass Edixhoven and Jean-Marc Couveignes, using étale cohomological methods. Kedlaya and Lauder-Wan also studied the Dwork approach from a computational viewpoint.
          %h2#necessary_tools Necessary tools
          %p
            Since the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be used is given by a non-linear equation, it is not directly adapted to the use of computational methods. One will thus need to extract linear invariants from
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} tangent (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            It seems that étale cohomological methods, that were originally used by Grothendieck and Deligne (and more recently, Laumon) to prove the full Weil conjectures, are not so easy to implement on a computer (see however the book of Edixhoven and Couveignes). It seems that “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic"} p-adic
            methods”, based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+differential+equation"} p-adic differential calculus
            and Fourier transform, and now completely developed by Berthelot, Lestum, Caro and Kedlaya (p-adic proof of the Weil-conjectures) are better adapted to computations (e.g., of L-functions over finite fields, i.e., characteristic polynomials of frobenius acting on cohomology).
          %p
            It thus looks like an important project to develop “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic"} p-adic
            methods” in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+spaces"} global analytic spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+cohomology"} absolute cohomology
            theory (related to the Chern character from K-theory to negative cyclic cohomology) and a
            %span.newWikiWord
              geometric cohomology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+cohomology"} ?
            theory (with characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            coefficients, e.g., in the full ring
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                global Fourier transform
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/global+Fourier+transform"} ?
          %p
            One of these cohomology theories (the absolute one) should give a natural way to study various conjectures on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+values+of+L-functions"} special values of L-functions
            and the other (the geometric one) should give a natural way to study the position of zeroes and poles of L-functions by spectral methods.
          %p
            The
            %span.newWikiWord
              geometric cohomology theory
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+cohomology+theory"} ?
            should also be (since it has characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            coefficients) related to the theory of motives and
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                global automorphic representations
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/global+automorphic+representations"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+variety"} arithmetic variety
          %h2#aims Aims
          %p
            The aim of arithmetic cryptography is to define a good
            %span.newWikiWord
              geometric cohomology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+cohomology"} ?
            theory for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+spaces"} global analytic spaces
            based on analytic methods and differential calculus that would allow the definition of
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-key_cryptography"} public key cryptography
            systems based on the datum of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+space"} global analytic space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and of (say) a part
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of the associated (maybe absolute) rational motive
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KISS_principle"} KISS principle
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
          %h2#possible_constraints_on_the_theory Possible constraints on the theory
          %p The constraints on such a theory would be the following:
          %ol
            %li
              %p get back the classical discrete logarithm elliptic public key cryptography method when one starts from an elliptic curve over a finite field (or maybe a corresponding weak formal scheme over the ring of p-typical Witt vectors).
            %li
              %p
                get back the original prime factorization public key cryptography method when one starts from
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %li
              %p
                It must be clearly stressed here that the proper theoretical setting for such a general theory may be very hard to develop, since it should involve the definition of a proper cohomology for the Riemann zeta function, that would allow a spectral proof of its functional equation (as in Tate’s thesis)
                %code and
                of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
                %span.newWikiWord
                  geometric cohomology
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+cohomology"} ?
                theory (even if ideas on the constraints that it should fulfill are widespread in the mathematical litterature, e.g., in Deninger’s work, in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
                litterature, in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} Langlands program
                and in the study of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Weil-étale cohomology
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Weil-%C3%A9tale+cohomology"} ?
                = succeed "-adic" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %li
              %p One may use the classical GRH conjectures (without proving them) to get the estimates analogous to the classical estimate of the number of primes smaller than a given prime. From this point of view, it seems that representation theoretic approaches may be more interesting.
          %h2#possible_methods Possible methods
          %p It is not at all clear that the following propositions will be conformal to the KISS principle. They may also be attacked by quantum computing methods, by higher dimensional generalizations of Shor’s algorithm, but one may hope that allowing the use of arbitrary arithmetic schemes may make the quantum computing methods more difficult to apply and/or implement in general. This also means that these propositions may be very hard to implement in practice.
          %p
            A starting point for crash-testing the compatibility of higher dimensional arithmetic cryptography with the
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KISS_principle"} KISS principle
            may be to test it in the finite characteristic case (with p-adic methods, say derived analytic spaces over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p
            may be understood as a discrete logarithm problem in the first (étale torsion) cohomology group of the curve, given by torsion points. One may try to generalize this problem to the higher dimensional situation by giving an algebraic cohomology class
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [d]=n.[c]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ([c],[d])
            and the message is the number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            One may also devise another “product type” approach to the definition of a public key: given two prime cohomology classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [d]
            (classes of irreductible subvarieties of a given codimension), compute their product class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [e]=[c].[d]
            in the cohomology ring. One may try, given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [e]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
